      "text": "Similar endpoints for user \naccount creation and login",
      "fontSize": 36,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Similar endpoints for user \naccount creation and login",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "Wx6E9_AhuuIjQ7mI5viin",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -40596.28039256917,
      "y": -13829.408103775924,
      "width": 1094.9036865234375,
      "height": 270,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#2f9e44",
      "backgroundColor": "#121212",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 2,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "aN",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 734747928,
      "version": 840,
      "versionNonce": 1520207669,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1776233590119,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "POST     /api/v1/urls              -> create short URL\nDELETE  /api/v1/urls              -> delete short URL\nGET    /{short_url}               -> redirect to long URL\nPOST  /api/v1/users               -> create user\nPOST  /api/v1/login                -> generate token and return\nPOST  /api/v1/logout              -> clear cookies",
      "fontSize": 36,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "POST     /api/v1/urls              -> create short URL\nDELETE  /api/v1/urls              -> delete short URL\nGET    /{short_url}               -> redirect to long URL\nPOST  /api/v1/users               -> create user\nPOST  /api/v1/login                -> generate token and return\nPOST  /api/v1/logout              -> clear cookies",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "YfuEnH42q9_KtGHYpFg_q",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -41890.351821140604,
      "y": -12604.987468855285,
      "width": 2786.470703125,
      "height": 9495,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b5Rx",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1906371688,
      "version": 3499,
      "versionNonce": 1146083477,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1776233590119,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "How are we going to generate  ids ?\n\n1. sha1 or md5     ->     sha1(longUrl) / or md5\n                                it generates 40 (sha) and 32 (md5) chars\n                                but we need 7 chars only \n\n                                if we select 1st 7 then there can be same for multiple urls\n\n\n2. to solve this issue : we can first check in db. if it exits then regenerate (appending extra chars)\n    downside is it will continue. untill it genereates unique : that might increases latency if there is too much colision and as scales it increases no of collisions\n\n     this increase issue of latency\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n3. use counter as id: this gurantees unique id generation every time\n\n    for this we can store counter in server. \n    but since we have large scale, we will have multiple servers... so we will not be able to manager consistent counter across multiple servers\n    \n    coz each of those services will have their own local counter      so there will again be collision\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n4. To solve this issue we can use redis counter (since redis is single threaded, it automatically hanldes concurrency)\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nwe have solve the issuee of id \n\nbut now we have SPOF since we have 1 redis \n\nif we want multiple redis instance  -  we can use redis cluster \nthen issue will be again same - each redis cluster will have it s own local counter\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n5. now if we notice by throughput\nwrite is 150m and read is 6b (6000m)   4ox more\n\nso encryption api might use 1 request \nand decryption api will be use 40 reqest as per requires\n\nso we can use individual services to for encription and decriotiponni to balance the load\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n6. to solve this final issue we will use zookeeper \n\nZookeeper is a distributed, open-source coordination service for distributed applications, \nproviding centralized services for configuration management, naming, synchronization, and group services, making it easy to build large clusters. \nUsed for: maintaining configuration information, naming, providing distributed synchronization, and providing group service etc\n\n\nzookeeper gives different range of ids to each servers to avoid collision\n\n\nzookeeper has 2 types of nodes : node which stores temporary data and another node that persists data even if it dead (we will store counter here)\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nNow we have unique ids : \n\n\n\n\n7. but how to conver id : 1 2 3 4 .. to exact 7 length string ?\n\ncurrent we have tiny.url/1, tiny.url/2, tiny.url/3, tiny.url/4.... \nthis is base 10 (coz we have 0-9 at each place)\n\n\nwe have 10 raise to 7 total possibiliteis <<<<<< 365B\n\n\n\n\nso to solve this issue we use base 62 : 0-9, a-z, A-Z\n\n62 raise to 7 is 3.5 T which is way more than >>>>>>> 365B\n\n\n",
      "fontSize": 36,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "How are we going to generate  ids ?\n\n1. sha1 or md5     ->     sha1(longUrl) / or md5\n                                it generates 40 (sha) and 32 (md5) chars\n                                but we need 7 chars only \n\n                                if we select 1st 7 then there can be same for multiple urls\n\n\n2. to solve this issue : we can first check in db. if it exits then regenerate (appending extra chars)\n    downside is it will continue. untill it genereates unique : that might increases latency if there is too much colision and as scales it increases no of collisions\n\n     this increase issue of latency\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n3. use counter as id: this gurantees unique id generation every time\n\n    for this we can store counter in server. \n    but since we have large scale, we will have multiple servers... so we will not be able to manager consistent counter across multiple servers\n    \n    coz each of those services will have their own local counter      so there will again be collision\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n4. To solve this issue we can use redis counter (since redis is single threaded, it automatically hanldes concurrency)\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nwe have solve the issuee of id \n\nbut now we have SPOF since we have 1 redis \n\nif we want multiple redis instance  -  we can use redis cluster \nthen issue will be again same - each redis cluster will have it s own local counter\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n5. now if we notice by throughput\nwrite is 150m and read is 6b (6000m)   4ox more\n\nso encryption api might use 1 request \nand decryption api will be use 40 reqest as per requires\n\nso we can use individual services to for encription and decriotiponni to balance the load\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n6. to solve this final issue we will use zookeeper \n\nZookeeper is a distributed, open-source coordination service for distributed applications, \nproviding centralized services for configuration management, naming, synchronization, and group services, making it easy to build large clusters. \nUsed for: maintaining configuration information, naming, providing distributed synchronization, and providing group service etc\n\n\nzookeeper gives different range of ids to each servers to avoid collision\n\n\nzookeeper has 2 types of nodes : node which stores temporary data and another node that persists data even if it dead (we will store counter here)\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nNow we have unique ids : \n\n\n\n\n7. but how to conver id : 1 2 3 4 .. to exact 7 length string ?\n\ncurrent we have tiny.url/1, tiny.url/2, tiny.url/3, tiny.url/4.... \nthis is base 10 (coz we have 0-9 at each place)\n\n\nwe have 10 raise to 7 total possibiliteis <<<<<< 365B\n\n\n\n\nso to solve this issue we use base 62 : 0-9, a-z, A-Z\n\n62 raise to 7 is 3.5 T which is way more than >>>>>>> 365B\n\n\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
